From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rumisle <why-excuse-quail@duck.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 02:31:00 +0800
Subject: core/session: conversation branches instead of destructive reverts

Committing a revert (sending after an edit or /undo) used to delete every message from the
boundary on. They are now parked as a branch hanging off the message before the boundary, and
GET /api/session/:id/branch lists the branch points visible from the current history (the
user message that shows the switcher and every alternative, oldest first). POST
/api/session/:id/branch/switch swaps a parked branch in for the messages after its anchor,
which are parked in turn (event session.branch.switched), so one session holds a tree of
conversations while the model always sees one linear history. Refused while the session runs.

Parked rows keep their ids and seqs, so a restored branch cannot collide with the rows left in
the session, and branches off messages inside a parked branch come back with it. They live in
_ocelot_branch, created on first use: the migrator leaves underscore-prefixed tables to
embedders, so there is no migration to conflict with upstream. Deleting a session deletes its
branches (foreign key).

Reverts no longer touch files: staging captures and restores nothing, so the conversation and
the working tree are independent (snapshots still feed the per-turn diffs). A revert staged by
an older version still puts its files back when cleared.

The shared client store (web app and TUI) keeps branch points per session and reloads the
messages when a branch is switched elsewhere.

Ocelot-Patch: core/message-branches

